-- title:
--   Theorem 3.2 — bounded DCAe sequences converge to a critical point
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $f,g,h,\varphi,L,l$ satisfy Assumptions 1 and 2, where Assumption 2 asks that the auxiliary function
--
--   $$
--   \Phi(x,y)=F(x)+\frac{(1+\delta)L}{2}\,D_\varphi(y,x)
--   $$
--
--   have the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz property at every point of $\operatorname{dom}\partial_L\Phi$, that $\nabla f,\nabla\varphi$ be Lipschitz on bounded sets, and that $h$ be differentiable with $\nabla h$ Lipschitz on bounded sets. Let $\delta\in(0,1)$ and let $(x^k)$ be a sequence generated by DCAe. If $(x^k)$ is bounded, then it converges to a critical point $x^*$ of (1):
--
--   $$
--   x^k\to x^*,\qquad \bigl[\nabla f(x^*)+\partial g(x^*)\bigr]\cap\partial h(x^*)\neq\emptyset .
--   $$
--
--   This is the global convergence of the whole sequence, as opposed to the subsequential statement of Theorem 3.1(c).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Every choice of $\beta_k$ satisfying (8), of $\xi^k\in\partial h(x^k)$ and of a minimizer of (9) is covered, from any $x^0$. No second-order differentiability of $\varphi$ is assumed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: E a finite dimensional real linear space with inner product ⟨·,·⟩ and norm ‖·‖ → any finite-dimensional real inner product space E; reason: the paper uses ⟨·,·⟩ and ‖·‖ on E; same instances
--   - encoding: g : E → (−∞, +∞] and F = f + g − h extended-real → g and F valued in EReal; reason: Lean convention; f and h are real so no ∞ − ∞ occurs; same instances
--   - encoding: x^{−1} := x^0 (initialization of Algorithm 1) → x : ℕ → E and prev x 0 = x 0, prev x (k+1) = x k; reason: the convention is built into prev; same runs
--   - reading: Assumption 1(b) g : E → R ∩ {+∞} → g : E → EReal, proper (never −∞, not identically +∞), lsc, convex; reason: R ∩ {+∞} is empty; the intended codomain is R ∪ {+∞}, as in §2 (p.4)
--   - reading: (9) prints ⟨Lφ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩ → ⟨L∇φ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩; reason: Lφ(y^k) is a number where a vector is needed; the text above Algorithm 1 (∇Lφ(y^k)) and the proof of Theorem 3.1 (L∇φ(y^k)) give the only well-typed reading
--   - reading: β_k is the extrapolation parameter satisfying (8); the proofs on pp.7 and 9 use 0 ≤ β_k < 1 → β_k any real satisfying (8); reason: Algorithm 1 imposes only (8); adding 0 ≤ β_k < 1 would be the weaker reading, and strong convexity of φ with (8) gives the bounds on ‖y^k − x^k‖ the proofs need
--   - reading: critical point: [∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*) ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅ (unclosed bracket, p.4) → ∃ ξ ∈ ∂h(x*), ξ − ∇f(x*) ∈ ∂g(x*), i.e. [∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*)] ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅; reason: this is the set the proof of Theorem 3.1(c) shows nonempty, (23) p.8
--   - encoding: pairs z = (x, y) ∈ E × E with the norm ‖z‖ → z ∈ WithLp 2 (E × E), ‖z‖² = ‖x‖² + ‖y‖²; reason: the paper's norm on pairs is Euclidean (its κ carries the √2 of Cauchy–Schwarz, p.9); Mathlib's E × E has the sup norm
--   - encoding: Φ satisfies the KL property at any point x ∈ dom ∂_LΦ, with η ∈ (0, ∞] → ∀ z, (LimitingSubdiff Φ z).Nonempty → HasKLProperty Φ z, with η a positive real; reason: dom ∂_LΦ is where ∂_LΦ is nonempty; a desingularizer on [0, η) restricts to [0, η') for finite η', so finite η loses nothing
--   - encoding: ∇f, ∇φ, ∇h Lipschitz continuous on any bounded subset of E → for every bounded S some K with LipschitzOnWith K on S; reason: same meaning
-- source:
--   Phan, Le Thi, DCA based algorithm with extrapolation for nonconvex nonsmooth optimization, arXiv:2106.04743v1, Theorem 3.2, p.8

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_Assumption1
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_IsDCAeRun
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_IsCriticalPoint
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_Assumption2

open Filter Topology

namespace DCAExtrap

/-- **Theorem 3.2** (Phan–Le Thi, arXiv:2106.04743v1, p.8). Let `{x^k}` be a sequence generated by
DCAe. Suppose that Assumptions 1 and 2 are satisfied. If `{x^k}` is bounded, it converges to a
critical point of (1).

**Formalization Note.** `E` is any finite-dimensional real inner product space; `g`, `F` and `Φ`
are `EReal`-valued; pairs live in `WithLp 2 (E × E)`. A DCAe run allows any `δ ∈ (0, 1)`, any
`β_k` satisfying (8) (no range constraint), any `ξ^k ∈ ∂h(x^k)` and any minimizer `x^{k+1}` of
(9) (with `∇φ` restored in (9)), from an arbitrary `x^0 = x^{−1}`. No second-order smoothness of
`φ` is assumed. A critical point satisfies `[∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*)] ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅`. -/
theorem theorem_3_2 {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] {f : E → ℝ} {g : E → EReal} {h : E → ℝ} {φ : E → ℝ} {L l δ : ℝ}
    {x : ℕ → E} {β : ℕ → ℝ} {ξ : ℕ → E}
    (hA1 : Assumption1 f g h φ L l) (hA2 : Assumption2 f g h φ L δ)
    (hrun : IsDCAeRun f g h φ L l δ x β ξ) (hbdd : Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range x)) :
    ∃ xstar : E, Tendsto x atTop (𝓝 xstar) ∧ IsCriticalPoint f g h xstar := by sorry

end DCAExtrap
